[size=6][b]Cinematic Combat[/b][/size]

An OpenMW Lua mod that makes melee land harder: kills drop the world into slow motion, the camera shakes when a hit connects, the screen blows out like a camera caught by a sudden light, and Morrowind's spark effect is replaced with one made of streaks that fly the way they are actually moving.

Needs [b]OpenMW 0.51[/b] or newer and [b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/60256]Max Yari's Script Services (MSS)[/url][/b]. [b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/55508]OpenMW Impact Effects[/url][/b] is optional but strongly recommended - it is what decides where sparks happen.

[size=5][b]What it does[/b][/size]

[size=4][b]Slow motion[/b][/size]

Two of them, and every kill is checked against both:

[list]
[*]a [b]short[/b] dip, on every kill by default;
[*]a [b]long[/b] one, on the last enemy of a long fight by default.
[/list]

Each has its own trigger, chance, time scale and duration. A trigger is the loosest case it accepts - [i]every kill[/i], [i]last enemy of a fight[/i], [i]last enemy of a long fight[/i], or [i]never[/i] - and a kill qualifies if it is that case or a stricter one. Since the kill that ends a long fight is also an ordinary kill, both can qualify at once; when they do, [b]the longer one wins[/b]. A fight counts as long once it has been going for 20 seconds, which is itself a setting.

Knowing which kill ended the fight comes from the engine. OpenMW's combat music is driven by a script on every NPC and creature that watches its own combat targets and reports every change to the player - the same signal that starts and stops the battle playlist. This mod listens to it, so it knows who is still fighting you and how long they have been at it. It keeps working with combat music switched off.

[size=4][b]Camera shake[/b][/size]

Fires when a hit lands, decaying over a third of a second. Hits you take don't shake the camera unless you turn that up. With [b]Dynamic Camera[/b] installed it goes through that mod's extra angle API, so the two add up instead of overwriting each other.

[size=4][b]Kill flash[/b][/size]

An exposure blow-out, not a vignette: highlights bloom out and smear towards the middle of the screen, the darks fall away, the glare runs cold, and the whole frame lifts - a camera, or an eye, caught by a light it was not ready for. It snaps in over a couple of frames and takes most of a second to recover.

Nothing gets darker. The tone curve is a straight multiply, so every pixel comes out at least as bright as it went in, and the top of the range clips to white: on a dim interior frame that lifts the mean by half and blows out a third of the image. There is a [b]shadow crush[/b] knob for the look a real overexposed frame has, where the darks fall away as the highlights blow - it is off by default, because any amount of it darkens the darkest part of the screen, and in a Morrowind interior that is most of the screen.

It runs as two passes: a quarter resolution bright pass, which thresholds the image [i]after[/i] exposure so a dim room still blooms once it has been blown out, then a composite that spreads that buffer back over the image. Everything about it - glare amount, threshold, radius, streak length, exposure, shadow crush and tint - is a uniform you can tune live from the post processing HUD. It takes the same trigger setting as the slow motion, so it can be limited to the end of a fight.

[size=4][b]Sparks[/b][/size]

Morrowind spark effects are particles, and OpenMW draws NIF particles as camera-facing squares: it parses [font=Courier New]NiParticleRotation[/font] and then ignores it ([font=Courier New]nifloader.cpp[/font], "RC_NiParticleRotation // unused"), and there is no velocity-aligned particle mode. A stretched particle sprite would lie down in the same screen direction for every spark, which looks wrong.

So the sparks in this mod are not particles at all. Each one is a pair of quads crossed along its own axis, flying a ballistic arc that is [b]baked into keyframes[/b] by [font=Courier New]tools/make_sparks.py[/font], turned at every key to face the direction it is travelling at that moment - so the streak bends over as the spark falls. The crossed pair keeps it visible from any angle; both triangle windings are written so backface culling cannot hide it. They are white hot at the head, blue down the tail, and there are few of them: five to eight per hit rather than a cloud.

Every controller in the files clamps at its last key ([font=Courier New]flags = 8|4[/font]). NIF controllers cycle by default, so one that ends before the effect is cleaned up starts over - which showed up as a second burst appearing right at the end.

These are mesh replacers for [font=Courier New]meshes/e/impact/metalSpark.nif[/font], [font=Courier New]parrySpark.nif[/font] and [font=Courier New]shieldBlock.nif[/font], so they apply to whatever plays them - normally OpenMW Impact Effects. To go back to the originals, delete this mod's [font=Courier New]meshes/e/impact[/font] folder.

[b]Where sparks already happen:[/b] Impact Effects raycasts each swing, works out what it struck, and sparks off bare metal, shields, ice armour and [i]heavy armour on the body[/i] - it picks the helmet, cuirass or greaves by how high the hit landed, so it is not shield-only. Medium armour is the gap: it gets a sound and no sparks. [b]Sparks On Medium Armour[/b] in the settings fills that in.

[b]Impact lights:[/b] sparks throw a very short lived light where they appear (cold blue, ~0.1s), and hits on an actor that spark off nothing - flesh, cloth, light armour - get a weaker, warmer one that is meant to go unnoticed. Struck scenery gets the spark light but never the warm one.

Placing them takes some care. The engine's own hit position is not a contact point: [font=Courier New]getHitContact[/font] takes the victim's origin - their feet - and raises it by a [i]random[/i] 20% to 100% of their height, so a light placed there lands on the floor often enough to notice. The contact point Impact Effects raycast is used when it is available, then a ray through the middle of the screen, and only then the engine's guess.

Impact Effects does not always report, either: it bails out before its handlers whenever it cannot name a material, which includes any bare body part, so an unarmoured enemy never reaches the hook at all. Those hits are lit from the hit event instead.

NIF lights are not loaded by OpenMW, so these are real light records spawned by the mod, from a pool of three objects per colour that are parked disabled and reused rather than created and destroyed per hit.

[size=5][b]Settings[/b][/size]

Everything above is in Options → Scripts → [b]Cinematic Combat[/b], in four groups: Slow Motion, Camera Shake, Kill Flash and Impact Lights.

